  "identity": {
    "playing_style": "Pellegrini's Betis play a patient, possession-oriented 4-2-3-1 in which the playmaker (Isco, Lo Celso) links a double pivot to two direct wingers - Antony on the right (12 goals, 9 assists by April 2026) and Abde on the left - with a mobile No. 9 (Cucho, now joined by Parrott). The 2025/26 side drew 15 of 38 league games and conceded 48, finishing 5th on 60 points, but reached the Europa League quarter-finals and secured a first UCL appearance in 21 years via Spain's European performance spot.",
    "principles": [
      "stability: Pellegrini since 2020 (Copa del Rey 2022, Conference League final 2025, UCL 2026)",
      "balance experienced free/low-fee signings (Isco, Lo Celso, Bartra, Ceballos free 2026) with young resale assets (Bernal EUR 9.5-10.5m with EUR 50m clause, Parrott EUR 16m+4m to 2031)",
      "sell to fund: Altimira to Sporting EUR 18.2m; loan-to-buy where possible (Antony 2025, Amrabat returned)",
      "Nuevo Villamarin stadium rebuild - playing at La Cartuja meanwhile",
      "cantera pathway (Angel Ortiz, Pablo Garcia promoted)"
    ],
    "club_character": "One of Spain's best-supported clubs (60,000-plus season-ticket base) with a famously loyal, emotional fanbase ('Betis manque pierda') and a fierce city rivalry with Sevilla FC. Big-club atmosphere with mid-table historical resources; pressure is high but the boardroom has been unusually stable. Playing temporarily at the Estadio La Cartuja while the Benito Villamarin is rebuilt.",
    "ownership": "Real Betis Balompie SAD, publicly held with two dominant private shareholders: president Angel Haro (Wingenia holding) and vice-president Jose Miguel Lopez Catalan, ~19.5% each (~39% together); board members Haro, Lopez Catalan, Jose Maria Pagola and Ricardo Diaz re-elected at the shareholders' meeting.",
    "political_and_social_context": "Publicly documented: the club's shareholders' meeting approved growth accounts and progress on the Nuevo Villamarin project; Betis has run the Forever Green sustainability programme. The 2025/26 Copa quarter-final and Europa League ties were played at La Cartuja during the stadium works. No institution-level controversies found in this research.",
